        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>Objectives:</B><br>It is known that small animals show decreases in bone formation when the skeleton is unloaded and that humans develop acute disuse osteoporosis (bone loss) after prolonged periods of unloading.  The purpose of this experiment was to determine the response of human bone to a brief period of unloading in a bed rest model for space flight.<P>
<B>Approach:</B><br>Eight healthy men, similar in age to astronauts, were recruited for a 7-day bed rest study.  Their bones were labeled with antibiotics that have an affinity for bone tissue 7 days before bed rest, the first day of bed rest and the last day of bed rest.  An iliac crest bone biopsy was obtained 2-3 days after the last bed rest day and examined for rates of bone formation and resorption. Newer assays that serve as markers of bone metabolism (formation and resorption) were carried out on urine and serial blood specimens.   Diets were controlled and a metabolic study was conducted for a few days before, during 7 days bed rest and for up to 5 days after bed rest.  The urinary excretion of calcium, phosphorus, sodium and potassium was monitored and calcium regulating hormones in the circulation were monitored before, during and after bed rest. Statistical analyses estimated the changes in parameters measured by repeated measures ANOVA primarily and comparisons of bone histomorphometry with data from healthy normal volunteers the same age as the bed rest subjects. The concentration of calcium and phosphorus in sublingual cells before and after the bed rest period was measured.<P>
<B>Results:</B><br>The response of bone, as estimated by markers, revealed prompt increases in markers of bone resorption with significant increases by the 4th day of bed rest.  A marker of bone turnover in blood, osteocalcin, showed an increase within one day of bed rest, remained high for the 7 days of bed rest and returned to pre-bed rest values within 5 days of reambulation. This non-collagenous protein originating in bone proved to be the most sensitive indicator of the bed rest response.  We found average rates of bone formation were reduced in 6, unchanged in 1 and increased in 1.  The decreased was greatest in the most active subjects and the increase occurred in a swimmer.<br><br>There was an increase in the urinary excretion of calcium and sodium evident the 2nd bed rest day and continuing for the entire week.  Reduced excretion of phosphorus and 3', 5' cyclic adenosine monophosphate on the first and second bed rest days was followed by an increase in serum phosphorus on the 6th bed rest day.  Serum ionized calcium; parathyroid hormone and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D were decreased by the 6th and 7th bed rest day.  We concluded that the response of the bone and the calcium endocrine system of healthy men after only 7 days was similar to results in longer bed rest studies and emphasized the responsiveness of the adult human skeleton to the biomechanical stimuli induced by changes in activity and/or position.  <br><br>The concentration of calcium and phosphorus in sublingual cells, representative of epithelial soft tissue, showed significant increases from 81±13 to 109±13 for calcium and 1180±98 to 1577±187 Exa units after 7 days bed rest, a phenomenon that was later shown to be prevented by exercise in a longer 30 day bed rest study.
        </div>

